From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 05:53:39 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] dt-bindings: gpio: apple,d2365-pmic-gpio: Allow events on
 the D2207

The D2207 has the same kind of PMU event block as the D2365, with 112
events instead of 240 and PMIC GPIO line N at event 0x18 + N instead of
0x30 + N. Let its node be an interrupt controller with the PMU line as
its interrupt, and let it list wake-only GPIO events from its own range
(0x18-0x2c, the iPad Smart Cover halls on lines 10 and 12 being 0x22 and
0x24). The line interrupt is the one after the last event, 112 on the
D2207. The D2333 stays without events.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 .../bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml  | 61 ++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+), 21 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
index a962bb0..978a5a2 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
@@ -15,20 +15,21 @@ description: |
   respectively. The controller is accessed through the regmap exported by the
   parent Apple I2C PMIC device.
 
-  The D2365 also latches 240 PMU events (charger, ADC, GPIO edges and
-  others) in one read-to-clear block and signals them on a single active-low
-  line to the AP. As an interrupt controller the node owns that block: the
-  interrupt specifier is the PMU event number and a flags cell. PMIC GPIO
-  line N is event 0x30 + N, so GPIO consumers get their interrupt through the
-  GPIO specifier as well. Events are latched; what triggers a GPIO event is
-  the line configuration left by the boot firmware, so only edge flags are
-  accepted.
-
-  Interrupt 240, one past the last event, is the event line itself. Its
-  consumers run on every assertion of the line, before the event block is
-  read, so that a consumer can act before the I2C read that tells which
-  event it was (the Lightning charger withdraws its input-current grant
-  there).
+  The D2207 and D2365 also latch 112 and 240 PMU events respectively
+  (charger, ADC, GPIO edges and others) in one read-to-clear block and
+  signal them on a single active-low line to the AP. As an interrupt
+  controller the node owns that block: the interrupt specifier is the PMU
+  event number and a flags cell. PMIC GPIO line N is event 0x18 + N on the
+  D2207 and 0x30 + N on the D2365, so GPIO consumers get their interrupt
+  through the GPIO specifier as well. Events are latched; what triggers a
+  GPIO event is the line configuration left by the boot firmware, so only
+  edge flags are accepted.
+
+  Interrupt 112 (D2207) or 240 (D2365), one past the last event, is the
+  event line itself. Its consumers run on every assertion of the line,
+  before the event block is read, so that a consumer can act before the I2C
+  read that tells which event it was (the Lightning charger withdraws its
+  input-current grant there).
 
 properties:
   compatible:
@@ -59,12 +60,13 @@ properties:
     minItems: 1
     maxItems: 24
     items:
-      minimum: 0x30
+      minimum: 0x18
       maximum: 0x47
     description: |
-      GPIO events (0x30 plus the line number) that end system sleep and have
-      no interrupt consumer, such as the iPad Smart Cover hall sensors on
-      lines 0 and 10 (events 0x30 and 0x3a). They stay masked while the
+      GPIO events (the first GPIO event plus the line number) that end system
+      sleep and have no interrupt consumer, such as the iPad Smart Cover hall
+      sensors on D2365 lines 0 and 10 (events 0x30 and 0x3a) or D2207 lines
+      10 and 12 (events 0x22 and 0x24). They stay masked while the
       system is awake, where the line is read through the GPIO instead, and
       are unmasked only while it sleeps: a hall sensor drives the PMU line on
       every change of the magnet field. They cannot be mapped as interrupts.
@@ -82,15 +84,32 @@ allOf:
   - if:
       properties:
         compatible:
-          enum:
-            - apple,d2207-pmic-gpio
-            - apple,d2333-pmic-gpio
+          const: apple,d2333-pmic-gpio
     then:
       properties:
         interrupt-controller: false
         "#interrupt-cells": false
         interrupts: false
         apple,wake-events: false
+  - if:
+      properties:
+        compatible:
+          const: apple,d2207-pmic-gpio
+    then:
+      properties:
+        apple,wake-events:
+          maxItems: 21
+          items:
+            maximum: 0x2c
+  - if:
+      properties:
+        compatible:
+          const: apple,d2365-pmic-gpio
+    then:
+      properties:
+        apple,wake-events:
+          items:
+            minimum: 0x30
 
 additionalProperties: false
 
-- 
